\begin_layout Title
The role of demographic feedback on the evolution of developmental windows
\end_layout

\begin_layout Part
Main Manuscript
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Introduction
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
There is an ongoing mass extinction event and,
 as many of the relevant anthropogenic stressors continue to worsen—
not least but not limited to rising global temperatures and the consequent risks of climate system breakdown (CITE)—
biological models have an important job to do in predicting the demographic and adaptive responses of species and ecosystems to these changing conditions.
 There remains much we can do,
 in principle,
 to improve these models and so generate more robust and informative guidance for policy makers.
 For instance,
 models of demographic change have yet to synthesise reports from the evolutionary theory literature of the great speeds (within ecological timescales) at which adaptation can occur and of the important role of plasticity and agency of individuals in directing this adaptation (CITE).
 Benton (2012) (CITE),
 shows how the complexities of real populations resulting from individual-level genetic variation and plasticity can play a strong role in population responses.
 We need more models like this,
 elucidating what variables are important in population dynamics,
 in order to develop appropriate theory,
 generate accurate empirical predictions,
 and present robust and effective policy guidance (CITE).
 Research within behavioural ecology has increasingly focused on the roles of uncertainty,
 state-dependent decision making,
 and learning;
 with implications for the evolution of various behavioural and developmental strategies (CITE CITE CITE),
 but their roles in population dynamics and the implications for us remain largely speculative (CITE).
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Main question with examples
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The main question is,
 given the predictability and severity of food limitations in an organism's environment,
 over what length of time do individuals invest in developing higher reproductive quality at an opportunity cost to investing in growth to exploit periods of abundant food?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Developmental windows are periods in development where sensitivity to cues is heightened.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Walasek et al.
 (2021),
 Walasek et al.
 (2022) find that the timing of when sensitive periods emerge depends on the reliability of cues increases across ontogeny,
 and their results suggest that both information and phenotypic states are modeled,
 with incremental development in fluctuating environments favouring residual development across ontogeny.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Further,
 Walasek et al (2024) review current theory and empirical literature and suggest future models could better integrate with empirical research by incorporating phenomena found in empirical systems into our models,
 and design more models that can be tailored to the life histories of specific species.
 They encourage for instance that models that incorporate the life history trade-offs we see in the development of alternative phenotypes in bulb mites (between scramblers and fighters) (Smallegange,
 2023;
 Smallegange et al.,
 2019),
 and it happens that authors interested in these taxa are also interested in bridging the gap in research between demographic modelling and agency with respect to development shaping demography (Smallegange ).
\end_layout

