\begin_layout Section
The Model Outline
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We model a large population of asexual individuals,
 distributed in a metapopulation across habitat patches of equal size and quality,
 but each with a population density state with transition probabilities determined by the local population's growth rate.
 Each individual has three state dimensions—
size (
\begin_inset Formula $s$
\end_inset

),
 patch information (
\begin_inset Formula $\pi$
\end_inset

),
 and reproductive quality (
\begin_inset Formula $m$
\end_inset

)—
upon which their developmental strategy is dependent,
 
\begin_inset Formula $x(s,\pi,m)$
\end_inset

,
 which determines the fraction of ingested energy allocated to developing greater reproductive quality over growth to increase their size state (and so ultimately the local population growth rate).
 Size determines whether or not an individual is mature given they can only reproduce when they reach their maximum size 
\begin_inset Formula $S$
\end_inset

.
 An individual's patch information state gives them the subjective probabilities regarding each possible population density state of their habitat patch.
 Finally,
 an individual's reproductive quality state 
\begin_inset Formula $m$
\end_inset

 determines their rate of reproduction at maturity.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We assume mutations are rare and of small effect such that effectively all individuals across the metapopulation have the same state-dependent developmental strategy (henceforth referred to as the resident strategy).
 Given the resident strategy we then do a forward projection using a Markov chain until demographic stability is reached at the metapopulation level (i.e.
 among patches).
 Given the probability distribution of population density states of patches,
 and assuming individuals have complete information as to this probability distribution and of the transition probabilities between patch pop.
 density states,
 we find the best response strategy for an invading individual (i.e.
 the developmental strategy that maximises their expected fitness).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
The Life Cycle and Demographic Feedback
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We model the life cycle of both individuals and of the patches they reside in (see figure 1).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Newborns disperse into random patches,
 with accurate prior information as to the probability distribution over patch population density states,
 and causing transitions in patch population density states.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Individuals forage,
 with food availability being a random variable,
 normally distributed,
 with mean determined by the population density.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Individuals update their patch information state given informativeness of food availability.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Immature individuals allocate a proportion of their ingested energy towards increasing their future reproductive quality given their developmental strategy,
 the rest of the energy goes towards maturation (increasing their size).
 Mature individuals do not grow,
 investing all ingested energy towards current reproduction.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Individuals die given a baseline probability of death from exogenous factors and given perturbations in patch quality (e.g.
 disease outbreaks) influenced by the patch population density state,
 and in turn causing transitions of patch states to lower population densities.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Individuals that are of maximum size are mature and reproduce at rate determined by their reproductive quality state.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
State dimensions
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Patch population density state,
 
\begin_inset Formula $n\in\{0,1\}$
\end_inset

,
 corresponding to low and high population density states respectively.
 We assume that 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

 determines the probability of patch quality decline and determining the availability of food.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Size state,
 
\begin_inset Formula $s\in\{1,2,...,S\}$
\end_inset

,
 where 
\begin_inset Formula $S$
\end_inset

 is their size at maturity.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Patch belief state,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\pi=\{0,1/I,2/I,...,(I-1)/I,I/I\}$
\end_inset

,
 is an individual's implicit and subjective probability that the patch population density state is high,
 where 
\begin_inset Formula $L$
\end_inset

 determines the resolution of the belief state.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Reproductive quality state,
 
\begin_inset Formula $m\in\{0,1/M,2/M,...,M/M\}$
\end_inset

,
 corresponding to the quality of an individual's physiology and so the mass of their offspring.
 Further,
 any investment in quality above the quality threshold 
\begin_inset Formula $m_{switch}$
\end_inset

,
 results in an individual developing an alternative fighter phenotype,
 giving additional reproductive benefit 
\begin_inset Formula $r_{fighter}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

