\begin_layout Subsection
Patch density states
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Here we describe pseudocode for calculating the demographically stable probability distribution of population densities across patches and of the transition probabilities between patch density states.
 Patches have a population size state 
\begin_inset Formula $n\in\{0,1\}$
\end_inset

.
 The actual population size for any given state 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{n}$
\end_inset

 is defined as
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
q_{n}=q_{min}\cdot(n+1)\label{eq:}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{min}$
\end_inset

 is the maximum population size of any patch.
 Then the probability distribution over patch states in the population is defined as
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\eta=P(q_{n}),\label{eq:-50}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

which will emerge from actions of individuals using the resident developmental strategy.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Physiology of newborns
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Ideally,
 individuals would be born with precise and accurate prior information regarding the probability distribution over the population density states of patches.
 Individuals would then update their information state given cues for the population density of their patch which come from the availability of food,
 see section 1.2.
 Individuals are born at size state 1 and reproductive quality state 0,
 and arrive at their respective patches with a prior belief state over the local population density of
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\pi\approx\tilde{\eta},\label{eq:-3}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
