\noun default
\color inherit
 was the true proportion of patches that are of density state 
\begin_inset Formula $n=1$
\end_inset

 in their evolutionary history,
 emerging from the previous resident strategy and updated with each subsequent invasion (see section 5.2).
 Therefore,
 in subsequent invasions to form a discrete grid of states that we can solve a Markov decision process for we need interpolate each 
\begin_inset Formula $\pi$
\end_inset

 onto a grid of possible discrete probability values,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\pi=i\in\{0/I,1/I,2/I,...,I/I\}$
\end_inset

,
 where 
\begin_inset Formula $I$
\end_inset

 is an integer determining the fineness of the grid.
 The first step of interpolation is to find the floor of 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{\eta}$
\end_inset

 when it is scaled by the grid size,
 which will give us the nearest grid cell 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 below the continuous value
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
i=\lfloor\tilde{\eta}\cdot I\rfloor,\label{eq:-20}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

and subsequently the difference is the fractional part 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 defined as
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
f=\tilde{\eta}\cdot I-i.\label{eq:-21}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

We can then use the fractional part as a probability that the state should be interpolated to be 
\begin_inset Formula $(i+1)$
\end_inset

,
 finally giving us two discrete states and associated probabilities for defining the state distribution of migrants:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\tilde{\rho}(s=1,\pi=i,m=0)=1-f,\label{eq:-24}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\tilde{\rho}(s=1,\pi=i+1,m=0)=f\label{eq:-25}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Foraging
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Individuals observe cues for population density in the form of food availability.
 Food availability is a random variable influenced by the population density,
 all patches produce the same amount of food which must be shared,
 so for any given patch of population density 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{n}$
\end_inset

 individuals receive a food mass of 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
g\sim\text{Normal}(\frac{\bar{g}}{q_{n}},\sigma_{g})\label{eq:-14}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where parameter 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{g}$
\end_inset

 is the mean food mass production of any patch and parameter 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{g}$
\end_inset

 is the food mass standard deviation.
 However,
 as we are working with discrete mathematics,
 we must approximate this normal distribution using a pseudo-normal probability distribution over a grid of possible food mass values 
\begin_inset Formula $\boldsymbol{G}=\{0,1,...,G_{max}\}$
\end_inset

,
 with likelihoods for each realised food mass being 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\begin{array}{ccc}
L(g|q_{n})=\frac{1}{K}e^{-\frac{(g-\frac{\bar{g}}{q_{n}})^{2}}{2\sigma^{2}_{g}}} & \text{for} & g\in\boldsymbol{G},\end{array}\label{eq:-2}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $N$
\end_inset

 is a suitable integer that determines the fineness of the grid of values,
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $K$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
K=\underset{-G}{\overset{G}{\boldsymbol{\sum}}}e^{-\frac{(g-\frac{\bar{g}}{q_{n}})^{2}}{2\sigma^{2}_{g}}}.\label{eq:-15}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Observation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Given the food mass 
\begin_inset Formula $g$
\end_inset

,
 individuals observe the resulting nutrient data and update their population density information states (i.e.
 their belief states).
 Assuming Bayesian optimal updating,
 their posterior belief is defined 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall i$
\end_inset

 as
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\pi^{\prime}=\frac{\pi\cdot L(g|q_{1})}{\pi\cdot L(g|q_{1})+(1-\pi)\cdot L(g|q_{0})}.\label{eq:-16}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

The posterior belief state 
\begin_inset Formula $\pi^{\prime}$
\end_inset

 then needs to be interpolated onto a discrete grid of possible values of 
\begin_inset Formula $\pi$
\end_inset

 which (because it represent a probability distribution) lies on a unit simplex (i.e.
 all values are positive and they add up to 1),
 requiring a grid of barycentric co-ordinates.
 To this end,
 the first step is to decompose each 
\begin_inset Formula $\pi^{\prime}$
\end_inset

 into it's integer and fractional parts,
 with the integer part defined by
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
i=\lfloor\pi^{\prime}\cdot I\rfloor,\label{eq:-8}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

and subsequently the fractional part defined as
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
f=\pi^{\prime}\cdot I-i.\label{eq:-9}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This then gives the transition probabilities between discrete belief states
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
P(\pi^{\prime}=i|\pi,g)=1-f,\label{eq:-7}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
P(\pi^{\prime}=i+1|\pi,g)=f.\label{eq:-6}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Finally,
 for all other possible posterior co-ordinates on the belief grid,
 the transition probability is 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
P(\pi^{\prime}|\pi,g)=0.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Development
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Each individual,
 choose a developmental action given their state 
\begin_inset Formula $\{s,\pi,m\}$
\end_inset

,
 given the resident developmental strategy for that state 
\begin_inset Formula $x(s,\pi^{\prime},m)\in(0,G)$
\end_inset

.
 Their developmental action determines the threshold mass above which energy foraged goes to the development of phenotypes of greater reproductive quality (e.g.
 fighter morphology),
 generating a transition in their size state 
\begin_inset Formula $s$
\end_inset

 and their reproductive quality state 
\begin_inset Formula $m$
\end_inset

.
 Of the portion of energy allocated to quality,
 all energy below a size-dependent investment threshold goes to maintenance costs.
 Therefore,
 if investment in quality falls below this threshold,
 given their state-dependent strategy and their food mass,
 there is a discounted probability of survival such that if the individual has no energy to allocate to maintenance they die,
 where 
\begin_inset Formula $y(s)$
\end_inset

 is the size dependent maintenance cost 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
y(s,m)=\frac{s^{3}\cdot m}{S^{3}\cdot M}\cdot y_{max}\label{eq:-22}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where we assume maintenance costs scale by the volume of the individual and by the quality of their somatic cells and developmental systems (given their quality state 
\begin_inset Formula $m$
\end_inset

),
 and where 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{max}$
\end_inset

 is the maximum maintenance investment.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The resulting transition probabilities,
 
\begin_inset Formula $P(s^{\prime},m^{\prime}|x,s,\pi^{\prime},m,g)$
\end_inset

,
 between state 
\begin_inset Formula $\{s^{\prime},\pi^{\prime},m^{\prime}\}$
\end_inset

 and state 
\begin_inset Formula $\{s,\pi,m\}$
\end_inset

 given the resident developmental strategy 
\begin_inset Formula $x(s,\pi,m)$
\end_inset

 and discounted survival probabilities,
 are found be doing the following:
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
For all 
\begin_inset Formula $\{s,\pi,m,g\}$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
P(s^{\prime},m^{\prime}|x,s,\pi^{\prime},m,g)=0.\label{eq:-10}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
For all 
\begin_inset Formula $g$
\end_inset

 and for each state,
 calculate the changes in quality and size
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\Delta m=max(g-x(s,\pi^{\prime},m),0)/G\label{eq:-20-1}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta m$
\end_inset

 is the change in quality state of individuals after ingesting a food mass of 
\begin_inset Formula $g$
\end_inset

,
 and where
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\Delta s=max(g-\Delta m-y(s,m),0)/G,\label{eq:-19-1}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

is the change in size state of individuals after ingesting a food mass of 
\begin_inset Formula $g$
\end_inset

,
 given that all energy allocated to growth below the fixed and state-dependent maintenance cost threshold 
\begin_inset Formula $y(s,m)$
\end_inset

,
 below which all food mass is invested in maintenance.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Interpolate 
\begin_inset Formula $s+\Delta s,m+\Delta m$
\end_inset

 on their respective grids to generate integer parts 
\begin_inset Formula $s^{\prime}$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $m^{\prime}$
\end_inset

,
 and their respective fractional parts 
\begin_inset Formula $f_{s}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $f_{m}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
These changes then need to be interpolated onto the size and quality grids,
 and for all interpolated values we find the resulting discretised transitions and their respective probabilities
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
P(s^{\prime},m^{\prime},\text{survives}|x,s,\pi^{\prime},m,g)=(1-f_{s})\cdot(1-f_{m})\cdot min(1,\frac{g-(m^{\prime}-m)-(s^{\prime}-s)}{y(s,m)}),\label{eq:-11}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
P(s^{\prime},m^{\prime}+1,\text{survives}|x,s,\pi^{\prime},m,g)=(1-f_{s})\cdot(f_{m})\cdot min(1,\frac{g-(m^{\prime}+1-m)-(s^{\prime}-s)}{y(s,m)}),\label{eq:-11-1}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
P(s^{\prime}+1,m^{\prime},\text{survives}|x,s,\pi^{\prime},m,g)=(f_{s})\cdot(1-f_{m})\cdot min(1,\frac{g-(m^{\prime}-m)-(s^{\prime}+1-s)}{y(s,m)}),\label{eq:-11-2}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
P(s^{\prime}+1,m^{\prime}+1,\text{survives}|x,s,\pi^{\prime},m,g)=(f_{s})\cdot(f_{m})\cdot min(1,\frac{g-(m^{\prime}+1-m)-(s^{\prime}+1-s)}{y(s,m)}).\label{eq:-11-3}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Demographic stochasticity
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Every patch undergoes stochastic perturbations in population density,
 influenced by patch population density (e.g.
 the local extinction of a prey species).
 We assume that the probability of death is equal for all individuals so there is no change in the distribution of individual states within patches as a result of these perturbations.
 We define the resulting transition probabilities as 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
P_{stoch}(q_{0}|q_{0})=1-\gamma_{low}\label{eq:-12}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
P_{stoch}(q_{1}|q_{0})=\gamma_{low}\label{eq:-13}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
P_{stoch}(q_{0}|q_{1})=\gamma_{high}\label{eq:-18}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
P_{stoch}(q_{1}|q_{1})=1-\gamma_{high}\label{eq:-19}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
where the parameters 
\begin_inset Formula $\gamma_{low}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\gamma_{high}$
\end_inset

 give the rates of transition up from low patch density states and down from high density states respectively.
 We assume 
\begin_inset Formula $\gamma_{high}>\gamma_{low}$
\end_inset

,
 as high density patches are more susceptible to famine and disease outbreaks.
 Therefore,
 given an individual's information state after foraging 
