\begin_layout Subsection
Reproduction
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Adults,
 individuals of size state 
\begin_inset Formula $s=S$
\end_inset

,
 reproduce asexually at a rate dependent on their reproductive quality state.
 We here define 
\begin_inset Formula $R(s,m,n)$
\end_inset

,
 the expected reproductive rate of an individual of state 
\begin_inset Formula $\{s,m\}$
\end_inset

 in a patch of density 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{n}$
\end_inset

,
 as 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
R(s,m,n)=\begin{cases}
\text{if \ensuremath{s\geq S} and }m\geq m_{switch}: & \sum_{g}L(g|q_{n})\cdot g\cdot(r_{b}+m\cdot r_{m}+r_{fighter})\\
\text{if \ensuremath{s\geq S} and }m<m_{switch} & \sum_{g}L(g|q_{n})\cdot g\cdot(r_{b}+m\cdot r_{m})\\
\text{else}: & 0
\end{cases}\label{eq:-1-1}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where S is their size at maturity,
 
\begin_inset Formula $m_{switch}$
\end_inset

 is the threshold investment in quality above which the fighter polyphenism is expressed,
 
\begin_inset Formula $r_{b}$
\end_inset

 is the baseline reproductive rate,
 
\begin_inset Formula $r_{m}$
\end_inset

 is the reproductive benefit of greater somatic quality,
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $r_{fighter}$
\end_inset

 is reproductive benefit of expressing the fighter polymorphism allowing them to extract auxiliary resources for reproduction.
 To make solving the model tractable we must assume the fighter phenotype does not influence competition for the shared limited resource represented by 
\begin_inset Formula $g$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Demographic projection and stability
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Initialisation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We will remind you of the definitions of the following submodels and parameters as we go but we summarise them here too for references.
 We have the following fixed parameters that must be initialised for a given run of the model:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $S$
\end_inset

:
 The maximum size state and the size at which maturity is reached.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $M$
\end_inset

:
 The maximum quality state.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $G$
\end_inset

:
 The maximum food mass digestible by any given individual.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $\gamma_{H}$
\end_inset

:
 The transition probability from a high (
\begin_inset Formula $n=1$
\end_inset

) to a low (
\begin_inset Formula $n=0$
\end_inset

) population density patch state due to demographic stochasticity and patch perturbations.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $\gamma_{L}$
\end_inset

:
 The transition probability from a low to a high population density patch state due to demographic stochasticity and patch perturbations.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda$
\end_inset

:
 The baseline probability of death due to exogenous factors between growth cycles.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $\mu$
\end_inset

:
 The probability of offspring dispersing to another patch.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $y_{max}$
\end_inset

:
 The maximum maintenance investment.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $q_{min}$
\end_inset

:
 The minimum population density of a patch assuming immigration from exogenous source populations and the minimum availability of food.
\end_layout

