\begin_layout Standard
There are then multiple submodels that we have defined so far and that vary in the number of times they must be run.
 We first initialise the following submodels which need be initialised only once and in the following order as outlined above:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
\begin_inset Formula $q_{n}$
\end_inset

:
 The population density of a patch of population density state 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
\begin_inset Formula $\eta$
\end_inset

:
 The proportion of patches that are of population density state 
\begin_inset Formula $n=1$
\end_inset

,
 which will be initialised as 
\begin_inset Formula $\eta=0.5$
\end_inset

,
 and subsequentially updated by the actions of the metapopulation.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
\begin_inset Formula $L(g|q_{n})$
\end_inset

:
 The likelihood of receiving a food mass of 
\begin_inset Formula $g$
\end_inset

 given that an individual is a population density of 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{n}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
\begin_inset Formula $P(\pi^{\prime}|\pi,g)$
\end_inset

:
 The transition probability from belief state 
\begin_inset Formula $\pi$
\end_inset

 to state 
\begin_inset Formula $\pi^{\prime}$
\end_inset

 given Bayesian updating upon receiving the mass cue 
\begin_inset Formula $g$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
\begin_inset Formula $P_{stoch}(q_{n}|q_{o})$
\end_inset

:
 The transition probability of patch densities,
 from 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{o}$
\end_inset

to 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{n}$
\end_inset

,
 given stochastic perturbations in population density,
 influenced by patch population density.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
\begin_inset Formula $R(s,m,n)$
\end_inset

:
 The expected reproductive rate of an individual of state 
\begin_inset Formula $\{s,m\}$
\end_inset

 in a patch of density 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{n}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Then,
 in the first instance and for each subsequent change in the resident strategy (between invasions,
 see section 5) we must also update all submodels that are dependent on the resident strategy and the evolving priors:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{\eta}$
\end_inset

:
 The evolved prior for the proportion of patches that are of population density state 
\begin_inset Formula $n=1$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{P}(q_{n}|q_{o})$
\end_inset

:
 The evolved priors for the transition probabilities between patch population density states,
 from each possible prior density state 
\begin_inset Formula $o\in\{0,1\}$
\end_inset

,
 to each possible posterior density state 
\begin_inset Formula $n\in\{0,1\}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
\begin_inset Formula $x(s,\pi^{\prime},m):$
\end_inset

 The resident developmental strategy,
 defining a threshold input food mass above which all excess food mass is invested in the development of a greater quality state.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate

